[{"category":"section","location":"#WolframCA","page":"WolframCA","text":"(Image: Description)\n\nA cellular automaton is a collection of \"colored\" cells on a grid of specified shape that evolves through a number of discrete time steps according to a set of rules based on the states of neighboring cells.  The rules are then applied iteratively for as many time steps as desired.\n\nmathworld.wolfram.com/CellularAutomaton\n\n(Image: Description)","title":"WolframCA"},{"category":"section","location":"#Elementary-CA","page":"WolframCA","text":"To generate an elementary cellular automaton, use\n\nca = CellularAutomaton(rule, init, gen)\n\nwhere rule is the Wolfram code (integer), init is a vector containing the initial starting condition and gen is the number of generations to be computed. For a single starting cell in the middle just omit the init vector.\n\nTo generate 15 generations of elementary cellular automaton of rule 90 use\n\nusing WolframCA\n\nca90 = CellularAutomaton(90, 16)\n\n                            #                                    \n                           # #                                   \n                          #   #                                  \n                         # # # #                                 \n                        #       #                                \n                       # #     # #                               \n                      #   #   #   #                              \n                     # # # # # # # #                             \n                    #               #                            \n                   # #             # #                           \n                  #   #           #   #                          \n                 # # # #         # # # #                         \n                #       #       #       #                        \n               # #     # #     # #     # #                       \n              #   #   #   #   #   #   #   #                      \n             # # # # # # # # # # # # # # # #","title":"Elementary CA"},{"category":"section","location":"#Totalistic-CA","page":"WolframCA","text":"For a more complex cellular automaton you can change the number of states k the cell can be and the radius r of neighbors that can influence the states. If k is changed to be larger than 2, a totalistic CA is computed where only the average value of all neighbors count. This can be done like this\n\nca = CellularAutomaton(993, 15, k=3)\n\n                        X                         \n                       XXX                        \n                      X# #X                       \n                     X     X                      \n                    XXX   XXX                     \n                   X# #X X# #X                    \n                  X     #     X                   \n                 XXX   ###   XXX                  \n                X# #X # X # X# #X                 \n               X      # X #      X                \n              XXX    ## X ##    XXX               \n             X# #X  #   X   #  X# #X              \n            X     X### XXX ###X     X             \n           XXX   X XX  # #  XX X   XXX            \n          X# #X XX###X## ##X###XX X# #X","title":"Totalistic CA"},{"category":"section","location":"#2-dimensional-CAs","page":"WolframCA","text":"Two dimensional cellular automaton (like Conway's Game of Life) can be created by\n\nca = CA2d(B, S, init, gen)\n\nwhere B and S are vectors that have the numbers of neighboring cells that define when cell is born or survives, init (matrix) is the initial starting condition and gen is the number of generations the CA is to be computed.\n\nGame of life is then run for 9 generations for e.g. a turbine pattern by typing\n\nca = CA2d([3], [2, 3], init, 9)\n\n1st step\n\n   ###### ##        \n   ###### ##        \n          ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##               \n   ## ######        \n   ## ######\n\n2nd\t       \n\n    ####            \n   #    # ##        \n   #    #   #       \n      ##    #       \n   ##    #  #       \n  #  #   #  #       \n  #  #    ##        \n  #    ##           \n  #   #    #        \n   ## #    #        \n       ####\n\n3rd\t     \n\n     ##             \n    ####            \n   # ## ## #        \n        ##  #       \n   ##  ##  ###      \n   #### #  ###      \n  #  #   #  #       \n ###  # ####        \n ###  ##  ##        \n  #  ##             \n   # ## ## #        \n       ####         \n        ##\n\n4th\n\n    #  #            \n        #           \n         ##         \n   # ##      #      \n   #  #   #         \n  #   # ###         \n #           #      \n    ### #   #       \n    #   #  #        \n #      ## #        \n    ##              \n      #             \n       #  #\n\n5th\n\n        ##          \n         #          \n    ###  ##         \n  ### #   #         \n  #    # ##         \n      # #           \n    ## #    #       \n    #   # ###       \n    ##  ###         \n     #              \n     ##\n\n6th\n\n        ##          \n     #              \n    # #  ##         \n  # # ###  #        \n  #  ######         \n     ## ##          \n    ######  #       \n   #  ### # #       \n    ##  # #         \n         #          \n     ##\n\n7th\t \n\n     #  # #         \n   ## # ###         \n    #      #        \n   ##     #         \n                    \n    #     ##        \n   #      #         \n    ### # ##        \n    # #  #\n\n8th\t                    \n\n    ## ## #         \n   ##  ## ##        \n           #        \n   ##               \n   ##     ##        \n          ##        \n   #                \n   ## ##  ##        \n    # ## ##\n\n9th\t                    \n\n   ###### ##        \n   ###### ##        \n          ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##     ##        \n   ##               \n   ## ######        \n   ## ######","title":"2 dimensional CAs"},{"category":"section","location":"#Running-Tests","page":"WolframCA","text":"To run tests, execute the following command from the root folder of the repository:\n\njulia tests/run_tests.jl","title":"Running Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"#Functions-Reference","page":"WolframCA","text":"","title":"Functions Reference"},{"category":"method","location":"#WolframCA.CellularAutomaton-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"WolframCA","text":"CellularAutomaton(N::Int, gen::Int; kvs...)\n\nQuick setup for one seed cell in the center as init.\n\nN is the Wolfram code. gen is the number of generations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.CellularAutomaton"},{"category":"method","location":"#WolframCA.CellularAutomaton-Tuple{Int64, Vector{Int64}, Int64}","page":"WolframCA","text":"CellularAutomaton(N::Int, init::Array{Int,1}, gen::Int; k::Int=2, r::Int=1)\n\nConstructor for 1-dimensional CellularAutomaton.\n\nN is the Wolfram code. init is the initial state. gen is the number of generations. k is the number of states. r is the neighborhood radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.CellularAutomaton"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.show-Tuple{IO, CA2d}","page":"WolframCA","text":"Base.show(io::IO, ca::CA2d)\n\nPrint CA to screen as CA2d\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.show-Tuple{IO, CellularAutomaton}","page":"WolframCA","text":"Base.show(io::IO, ca::CA2d)\n\nPrint CA to screen from CA as CellularAutomaton\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"image/png\")}, CA2d}","page":"WolframCA","text":"Render CA2d to a PNG image, stacking each generation's grid vertically,\nseparated by a blank gap, as in the text representation above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"image/png\")}, CellularAutomaton}","page":"WolframCA","text":"    Base.show(io::IO, ::MIME\"image/png\", ca::CellularAutomaton)\n\nRender CA to a PNG image (e.g. for Jupyter/Pluto/VS Code's plot pane), with each generation on its own row, as in the text representation above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"#WolframCA.cell_rgb-Tuple{CellularAutomaton, Integer}","page":"WolframCA","text":"cell_rgb(ca::CellularAutomaton, state::Integer)\n\nPacked 0x00RRGGBB color for a single cell's state: white when dead, black when alive, and gray for the second \"alive\" state of a k=3 totalistic CA. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.cell_rgb"},{"category":"method","location":"#WolframCA.eval_rule-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"WolframCA","text":"Evaluate life rules for the CA2d. Return an Int not a Bool \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.eval_rule"},{"category":"method","location":"#WolframCA.grid_to_image-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"WolframCA","text":"grid_to_image(ca, grid::AbstractMatrix)\n\nBuild a pixel buffer for a 2-dim grid of cell states, scaling each cell up to a CELLPIXELS x CELLPIXELS block so the image is visible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.grid_to_image"},{"category":"function","location":"#WolframCA.rule","page":"WolframCA","text":"Parse rule numbering according to the Wolfram code \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WolframCA.rule"}]
